WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.340
You're watching ColdFusion TV

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.620
Hi, Welcome to another Coldfusion video

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.040
Just 10 years ago, nobody would have ever thought electric vehicles would be in mass production

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.880
Yet alone change the Auto industry

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.220
Today most car manufacturers are thinking about all-electric vehicles

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.760
Volvo is even saying that they may ditch diesel cars altogether and switch to hybrid and electric cars

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:34.500
What was once a pipe dream and impractical is now a reality in the auto industry

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:39.820
But now in the latter part of the 2010s the electric revolution is taking to the skies

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:46.660
Battery Tech is just getting good enough to allow several companies to roll out their fully electric aircraft into production

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:51.260
In this quick video, we'll take a look at the state of the art of fixed wing electric aircraft

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:58.320
Earlier the this year, the German firm Siemens just demonstrated a powerful electric aircraft that broke two old records

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.160
The first was a speed record

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:06.460
It was the fastest electric aircraft flying at a straight-line speed of 340 kilometers an hour

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.940
It also broke a climbing record rising from the ground to 3 kilometers in just over 4 minutes

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.320
It was also the first electric aircraft to air tow

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:21.840
The aircraft and extra '330LE' was able to do this thanks to a new generation of electric motors from Siemens

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:25.280
Weighing just 50 kilograms, the motor can produce 260 kilowatts,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.820
about five times the power of comparable aircraft engines or about as much output as the original Tesla model S

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:38.680
Siemens plans to use this aircraft as a testbed for developing more in the future

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:47.100
Another company Pipistrel from Slovenia if getting ready to ship their fully electric light aircraft the 'Alpha Electro'

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:52.860
The Alpha Electro will be used for flight training purposes and takes 45 minutes to fully charge

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.720
The aircraft cuts overall costs of flight training by 70% from fuel savings

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.360
And the learning process is exactly the same as its petrol counterpart

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:09.060
Interestingly every tenth of a second, the system on the aircraft checks the batteries, monitoring their voltage and looking out for any faults

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:13.500
It can electronically disconnect parts of the battery if the system predicts a fault

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.820
Upon landing the propeller turns into a generator recharging about five percent of the battery

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:26.320
Yet another company, Aero Electric, is launching another all-electric plane called the 'Sun Flyer'

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.900
It has the same idea of reducing the cost of flight training

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.760
"This is really a history-making event

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.840
Aero Electric aircraft corporation has just unveiled a prototype of its new

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.300
all-electric Sun Flyer plane which could open up the skies to many more pilots"

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:50.920
By using electricity, they claim that the price goes down from $45 per flight hour down to $1 per flight hour, a real quantum leap

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.000
I think this use case makes the most sense for all electric air travel,

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.400
small planes, one to two passengers, and short trips from 30 minutes to 1.5 hours

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:05.780
It would be good for scenic aerial tours short charter flights, or pilot training

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.540
So what are the advantages and disadvantages of electric propulsion?

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.120
Well the advantages are as follows

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.300
It's quieter, mechanically more efficient, and doesn't have any direct emissions

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.220
The disadvantages is the battery

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.080
The bottleneck really is with the battery energy density

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.720
it's just not good enough yet for long distance flight

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:30.020
In addition to this, with traditional aircraft, the more fuel you burn the lighter the craft gets,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:33.380
But with electric aircraft, the batteries are essentially dead weight

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.340
This is where hybrid systems come into play

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.680
More so than cars, hybrid planes seem to be the sweet spot for air travel

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.800
They can be used for medium distances, flying for about two to four hours

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.960
Airbus is working on a hybrid plane to go on to production by 2030

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:57.000
This comes after successfully testing their own fully-electric aircraft dubbed the 'E-fan', a project that ran from 2014 to last month

00:03:57.860 --> 00:04:02.620
During its testing the E-fan was claimed to be the first electric plane to cross the English Channel

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:07.480
With help from Siemens, both Pipistrel and Airbus are in the works of creating hybrid motors

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.540
But the hybrid motor from Pipistrel is already in testing phases and makes about 200 kilowatts

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.200
So in conclusion, electric aircraft have been in the experimental stages for many decades

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.320
But were woefully underpowered and largely had the same issues as electric cars before Tesla got the ball rolling

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:29.120
Now practical electric aircraft are right around the corner

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.140
In the next year or two, they'll be fully in service but only for short-range low passenger volume situations

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.520
But it seems like hybrids aren't far off and this could open the door to low emission mid-range flights

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:44.860
with the benefits of both an electric motor and traditional power

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:47.180
And with that, that wraps up the video

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.720
This was just a quick look at the state of the art of electric fixed-wing aircraft

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:53.920
Thanks for watching, this has been Dagogo, you've been watching ColdFusion

